---
title: "App translation"
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

The NAHPU app shows its labels, buttons, messages, and settings in English,
Brazilian Portuguese, Spanish, or Indonesian. Users choose the language in
`Settings` → `Language`. English is the source language; the other languages
are in beta while they are reviewed.

## Start simple

The easiest way to improve the app translation is to use NAHPU in your
language and note anything that reads wrong. Leave a comment in the
[NAHPU translation spreadsheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1zG3vkcIgsfPMAal2lai876juXS3U_B4DHPEAxi1-Qko/edit?usp=sharing)
with:

- the screen where you saw the text,
- the text as it appears in the app,
- your suggested translation, and
- a short reason when the choice is not obvious, such as the term your
  colleagues or institution use.

A screenshot helps when it does not expose sensitive data. A NAHPU team member
will carry the change into the app.

## Translation rules

Follow the [Translation glossary](../glossary) so the app and the documentation
use the same term for the same concept. In addition:

- **Keep it short.** Labels must fit on phone screens. Prefer the shortest
  natural wording, especially for buttons, tabs, and chips.
- **Keep placeholders unchanged.** Text in braces, such as `{date}` or
  `{count}`, is replaced by the app. Copy it exactly; you may move it within
  the sentence.
- **Translate plural forms, not their structure.** In text such as
  `{count, plural, =1{1 file} other{{count} files}}`, translate only the words
  inside the inner braces. Keep `count`, `plural`, `=1`, and `other` as they
  are.
- **Keep names and standards.** Keep `NAHPU`, Darwin Core terms, file formats,
  and Latin names exactly as written in English.
- **Read the description.** Many English strings come with a note describing
  where the text appears. Use it to choose between meanings of the same word.

## Edit the translation files

Contributors who work with code can edit the translation files directly in the
[NAHPU app repository](https://github.com/nahpu/nahpu). Read the
[app code setup guide](../../code/setup) first.

{% filetree %}
- lib/l10n/
  - app_en.arb English source, with descriptions and placeholders
  - app_pt.arb Brazilian Portuguese
  - app_es.arb Spanish
  - app_id.arb Indonesian
  - app_localizations*.dart Generated, do not edit
{% /filetree %}

{% steps %}

1. Find the key in `app_en.arb` and read its `@key` description, if any.

2. Edit the same key in the translation file, for example `app_pt.arb`.

   ```json
   "commonRetry": "Tentar novamente",
   ```

   Do not rename, add, or remove keys in a translation file. Every translation
   must contain all English keys with the same placeholders.

3. Regenerate the localization code.

   ```bash
   flutter gen-l10n
   ```

4. Run the localization tests.

   ```bash
   flutter test test/localization
   ```

5. Run the app, switch to your language in `Settings` → `Language`, and check
   that the text fits and reads naturally on the screens you changed.

6. Submit a pull request with screenshots of the changed screens.

{% /steps %}

Each translation file starts with `"@@x-reviewed": false`. Leave it unchanged
unless a fluent speaker has reviewed the whole file against the English source.

## New English strings

When you add text to a screen, add the English string to `app_en.arb` instead
of writing it in the widget. Add a `@key` description when the meaning depends
on context, then add the same key to every translation file. A first draft may
come from a large language model, but mark it for review in your pull request
so a fluent speaker can check it.

## Add a new app language

A new language needs an ARB file in `lib/l10n/`, an entry in the `AppLanguage`
enum in `lib/services/localization/app_language.dart`, and the bundled
documentation folders under `assets/docs/`. Because the bundled Cookbook is
generated from this website, a new app language also needs a
[documentation translation](../documentation). Please contact a
[NAHPU team member](/teams) before starting.
